WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.203
- [Narrator] Breathe in, breathe out.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.540
With every breath, the body
is replenished and cleansed.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:13.800
A process made possible

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.513
by two of the body's most
important and delicate organs.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.350
The lungs are two major components

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.710
of the respiratory system.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.130
Soft, light, and elastic,

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.330
these organs are prime for taking in

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.973
and filling up with air.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.500
Each lung is subdivided into
compartments called lobes.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.180
The right lung has three,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.180
and the left lung,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.390
which is slightly smaller to accommodate

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.643
the positioning of the heart, has two.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.820
This compartmentalization provides

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.690
a fail-safe for the lungs,

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.840
so that if one lobe is damaged,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.903
the four other lobes may remain
intact and able to function.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.700
The lobes are also connected
to separate branches,

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.080
called bronchi, of the trachea,

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.310
which delivers air from
the nose and mouth.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.080
These branches provide the lobes

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.223
independent supplies of air.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.420
Within each lobe,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.940
the bronchi split off into
numerous smaller offshoots

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.060
that end in air sacs.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.620
Known as alveoli,

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.810
these sacs are responsible
for a crucial exchange of air.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.610
When a person inhales,
oxygen fills the alveoli.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.290
The alveolar walls, which
contain countless capillaries,

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:34.880
are so thin that the oxygen
inhaled can pass through them

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.090
and be absorbed by blood
cells within the capillaries.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.150
A similar transaction occurs
in the opposite direction.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.190
Carbon dioxide waste
transported in the bloodstream

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.090
can pass through the alveolar walls.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.603
From there, they are expelled
with every exhalation.

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.650
This exchange of gases,
known as respiration,

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.583
is what gives the
respiratory system its name.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:03.870
Because of their intake of air,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:06.870
the lungs are constantly
exposed to the elements,

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.993
and sometimes fall victim to them.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:13.220
Lung conditions may
develop, such as emphysema,

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.520
which for some is caused by the
inhalation of tobacco smoke.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.060
And pneumonia, which can be caused by

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.763
the inhalation of infectious organisms.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.140
All together, these
and other lung diseases

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:30.463
are the fourth-leading cause
of death in the United States.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.170
The lungs are delicate organs.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.120
But despite their vulnerability,

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.300
their anatomical design
allows them to stay strong

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.843
and play a vital role in fueling the body.

